## 4. Sensitivity — balanced panel, wealth observed in all three waves (n=5,739; 45.2%)

| Wave | white | black | Hispanic | W/B | W/H |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1985 | 3,800 | 1,600 | 300 | 2.38 | 12.67 |
| 1990 | 14,750 | 4,100 | 1,200 | 3.60 | 12.29 |
| 1996 | 42,600 | 9,075 | 2,400 | 4.69 | 17.75 |

**The sensitivity does not shrink the gap** (contrary to the pre-analysis expectation that
differential attrition, 52.6% of whites missing by 1996 vs 31.7% of blacks, would attenuate it):
ratios are similar or slightly larger in the balanced panel at every wave. The finding is not an
artefact of the available-case estimator.

## 5. Distribution overlap (why one median per group is not a clean separation)

IQR intervals overlap heavily at every wave, e.g. 1996 white 8,500-114,500 vs black 1,000-45,500 vs
Hispanic 0-17,495; the white 25th percentile sits *below* the black median in 1985, 1990 and 1996.
Upper tails are top-coded in the source file (2.0% of 1996 observations sit exactly at the
1,232,183 cap; 3.49% of whites vs 0.66% of blacks), so means are understated and were not used for
headline claims.

## 6. Omnibus test — with caveat

Kruskal-Wallis H = 625.9 (1985), 737.5 (1990), 1,080.9 (1996), all p < 1e-100. **Caveat: the file
carries no sampling weights and no strata/PSU identifiers, so these p-values and the bootstrap CIs
reflect within-file sampling noise only for this unweighted, non-nationally-representative tabulation**
(black and Hispanic cohorts were deliberately oversampled). They are reported for completeness, not
as inferential evidence about the US population.
